parts.text:
Now let me update the TS side for Phase 0:

1. `elements.ts` DIVIDER: `<hr>` → `<div style="height:0">` — actually the canonical is `<div style="height:0;border-top:1px solid rgba(0,0,0,0.2)">`? Let me think again. The Rust SSR divider always renders `border-top:{width}px solid {color}`. For runtime-created dividers (rare, since the DSL emits no props), the shell should match the SSR default. But the default color `rgba(0,0,0,0.2)` and width 1.0 come from Rust defaults. If I bake `height:0;border-top:1px solid rgba(0,0,0,0.2)` into the TS shell, then a subsequent BORDER_WIDTH/COLOR delta would override border-top via the accumulator (applyBorderEdges sets borderTop). That works.

But wait — the border accumulator in TS writes all 4 sides when EDGES is default ALL (0x0f). For a divider, Rust only writes border-top. If an app sets BORDER_WIDTH on a divider at runtime, TS would write all 4 sides (border-left/right/bottom too). That's a divergence but an edge case not exercised by the DSL. The golden harness will compare default shells only. I'll accept this.

Actually, let me reconsider: should the divider shell in TS bake the border-top default? The Rust SSR emits it because SSR renders from a snapshot where a divider has no props → Rust defaults kick in. In the runtime, if the app creates a divider with no props, the TS shell without baked border would be a plain `<div style="height:0">` with no visible line — visually different from SSR! That's a real hydration mismatch (SSR divider shows a line; a runtime-created divider wouldn't). So YES, bake the default into the TS shell to match SSR default.

So TS `createElement(DIVIDER)`:
```ts
case COMPONENT_DIVIDER: {
