parts.text:
happy-dom drops `calc()` and some `var()` values for properties that it validates with type-checking (padding, font-size, width), but keeps var() for color/gap. So this is a happy-dom validation artifact.

Given real browsers accept these, the runtime code is correct. The right move: make the runtime's DESIGN_TOKEN property application **write the raw value into the style attribute** (mirroring the SSR inline style), so both the browser and happy-dom preserve it, AND it matches the SSR output exactly. This is actually the most faithful approach — the SSR output is a style *attribute*; the client should produce the same attribute.

Let me implement `applyTokenRefProperty` to merge into the style attribute instead of CSSOM assignment for the tokenized properties. I'll rewrite the TOKEN_HANDLERS to map property → CSS property name, and apply via attribute merge.

Current TOKEN_HANDLERS maps property → (el, v) => el.style.X = v. Some have side effects (BORDER_COLOR → borderOf; SHADOW_COLOR → shadowOf). Those are accumulator cases.

New approach: for token refs, use a helper `setStyleCss(el, cssProp, value)` that:
- reads current style attribute
- removes any existing declaration for cssProp
- appends `cssProp:value`
- sets the attribute

But the accumulator cases (BORDER_COLOR, SHADOW_COLOR) need special handling — they feed into border/shadow recomposition, not a simple declaration. For those, token refs set the color; the accumulator then writes the full border/shadow. Hmm — in Rust, border color token ref → border_style() writes `border-{side}:{width} solid {color}`. The TS border accumulator: borderOf(el).color = v; applyBorderEdges(). applyBorderEdges writes via CSSOM `el.style.borderTop = ...` which happy-dom accepts (border shorthand). So those go through the accumulator path and serialize fine.

So I only need attribute-merge for the simple single-declaration token handlers. Let me restructure:

```ts
const TOKEN_HANDLERS: Record<number, (el: HTMLElement, cssProp: string, v: string) => void> = {
  PROP_COLOR: (el, p, v) => setStyleCss(el, p, v),
  ...
}
```

Actually, let me simplify: keep a table mapping property → css property name, and handle the two accumulator special cases separately.

Let me write:
```ts
// Tokenizable single-declaration properties: property → CSS property name.
const TOKEN_STYLE_PROPS: Record<number, string> = {
  [P.PROP_COLOR]: "color",
  [P.PROP_BACKGROUND_COLOR]: "background-color",
  [P.PROP_TINT]: "accent-color",
  [P.PROP_FONT_SIZE]: "font-size",
  [P.PROP_FONT_FAMILY]: "font-family",
  [P.PROP_FONT_WEIGHT]: "font-weight",
  [P.PROP_SPACING]: "gap",
  [P.PROP_PADDING]: "padding",
  [P.PROP_PADDING_TOP]: "padding-top",
  [P.PROP_PADDING_RIGHT]: "padding-right",
  [P.PROP_PADDING_BOTTOM]: "padding-bottom",
  [P.PROP_PADDING_LEFT]: "padding-left",
  [P.PROP_BORDER_RADIUS]: "border-radius",
  [P.PROP_OPACITY]: "opacity",
  [P.PROP_WIDTH]: "width",
  [P.PROP_HEIGHT]: "height",
};
```

And `applyTokenRefProperty`:
```ts
export function applyTokenRefProperty(el, property, tokenPath): void {
  const value = resolveTokenCssRef(tokenPath);
  if (property === P.PROP_BORDER_COLOR) {
    borderOf(el).color = value;
    applyBorderEdges(el);
    return;
  }
  if (property === P.PROP_SHADOW_COLOR) {
    shadowOf(el).color = value;
    recomposeShadow(el);
    return;
  }
  const cssProp = TOKEN_STYLE_PROPS[property];
  if (cssProp) {
    setStyleCss(el, cssProp, value);
  }
}
```

And `setStyleCss`:
```ts
function setStyleCss(el: HTMLElement, cssProp: string, value: string): void {
  const decls = (el.getAttribute("style") ?? "")
    .split(";")
    .map((s) => s.trim())
    .filter((s) => s.length > 0)
    .filter((s) => !s.toLowerCase().startsWith(`${cssProp}:`));
  decls.push(`${cssProp}:${value}`);
  el.setAttribute("style", decls.join(";"));
}
```

This writes the raw `padding:calc(var(--pl-space-base) * 2)` into the attribute — happy-dom preserves attribute values verbatim (as it does for innerHTML parse). And real browsers parse it correctly. This is faithful to SSR.

But there's a risk: mixing attribute-set styles and CSSOM-set styles on the same element. E.g., a node has shell `display:flex` (CSSOM) and later a token ref for SPACING (attribute). The attribute now has `display:flex;gap:calc(...)`. But the CSSOM cache — when the attribute is set, does happy-dom update `el.style.display`? In browsers, setAttribute("style") reparses into CSSOM. In happy-dom, likely yes. Subsequent CSSOM reads/writes work. For the conformance test, only getAttribute("style") matters, which reflects both. Should be fine.

Actually — wait, there's an ordering concern in the test: the runtime applies token refs in batch order. For node 3 ("Spaced"), the batch sets padding token ref. The runtime el has shell styles? No — a TEXT span has no shell styles. So attribute = "padding:calc(var(--pl-space-base) * 2)". getAttribute("style") returns that. And the SSR span has `style="padding:calc(var(--pl-space-base) * 2);"`. normalizeStyle both → `padding:calc(var(--pl-space-base) * 2)`. 

But hold on — the SSR side when parsed via `host.innerHTML = ssr`: happy-dom preserves the raw style attribute. getAttribute("style") on the parsed span returns `padding:calc(var(--pl-space-base) * 2);` (raw). normalizeStyle splits on ";" → one decl. Good.

But for node 5 (button "Go") with background-color token ref AND class pathland-button: runtime sets style attribute `background-color:var(--pl-color-primary)`. SSR: `style="background-color:var(--pl-color-primary);"`. Match.

Also — important: the COLOR node in the layout scenario has both background-color (via my new PROP_COLOR special-case → CSSOM) and color (CSSOM). And the CSSOM serialization gives `rgba(255, 0, 0, 1.000)` vs SSR `rgba(255,0,0,1)`. The layout Expected shows `rgba(255,0,0,1)` (SSR raw) and Received shows `rgba(255, 0, 0, 1.000)` (happy-dom CSSOM). These differ in spacing and the alpha `1.000` vs `1`! So my normalizeStyle must ALSO normalize rgba. Ugh.

Look at the layout failure: Expected `background-color:rgba(255,0,0,1)` vs Received `background-color:rgba(255, 0, 0, 1.000)`. So I need to normalize rgba formatting: collapse spaces and trailing zeros in alpha, and format as 1/0.

Let me add rgba normalization to normalizeStyle: for values matching `rgba(r, g, b, a)`, normalize to `rgba(r,g,b,a)` where a is `0` or `1` (or trimmed). happy-dom writes `1.000`; Rust writes `1` and `0.5`. So: `a = Number(a)` then if integer, drop decimals.

Regex: `/rgba\(([^)]*)\)/`. Split by comma, trim, format alpha: `const a = parseFloat(x); const alpha = Number.isInteger(a) ? String(a) : String(Math.round(a*1000)/1000)`.

Actually — better: instead of formatting in normalizeStyle, I could make the runtime set literal colors via the style attribute too (matching SSR exactly). But the runtime uses CSSOM for colors (applyProperty sets el.style.color = argbToRgba(...)). argbToRgba in format.ts — let me check what it outputs. It probably outputs `rgba(r,g,b,a)` with the Rust format. But happy-dom re-serializes it as `rgba(255, 0, 0, 1.000)`. So the CSSOM serialization reformats. The SSR string keeps the raw. So I MUST normalize rgba in the test.

Let me add rgba normalization to normalizeStyle:
```ts
value = value.replace(/rgba\(([^)]*)\)/g, (m, inner: string) => {
  const parts = inner.split(",").map((p) => p.trim());
  if (parts.length === 4) {
    const r = parts[0];
    const g = parts[1];
    const b = parts[2];
    const a = Math.round(parseFloat(parts[3]) * 1000) / 1000;
    return `rgba(${r},${g},${b},${a})`;
  }
  return m;
});
```
This handles `rgba(255, 0, 0, 1.000)` → `rgba(255,0,0,1)` and `rgba(255,0,0,1)` → unchanged. And `rgba(0,0,0,0.2)` → `rgba(0,0,0,0.2)`.

Also the flex longhand collapse needs to handle `flex:1 1 auto` from the COLOR shell. My current collapse: `flex-grow:1;flex-shrink:1;flex-basis:X` → if X=="0%" → "flex:1", else `flex:1 1 ${basis}`. For COLOR: basis="auto" → `flex:1 1 auto`. Matches SSR `flex:1 1 auto`. But wait — does happy-dom serialize the COLOR shell's `el.style.flex = "1 1 auto"` as longhands `flex-grow:1;flex-shrink:1;flex-basis:auto`? Probably yes (like flex:1). My collapse handles it. But the layout Received shows `flex:1 1 auto` already?? Looking at Received layout: `<div data-pathland-id=3 style:align-self:stretch;background-color:rgba(255, 0, 0, 1.000);color:rgba(255, 0, 0, 1.000);flex:1 1 auto></div>` — so happy-dom serialized `flex:1 1 auto` as-is (not expanded). Interesting — so `flex` shorthand with "1 1 auto" is preserved but `flex:1` is expanded? Inconsistent happy-dom behavior. My collapse handles the expanded case (`flex:1` → flex-grow/shrink/basis 0%); for `flex:1 1 auto` both sides keep the shorthand. Fine.

But wait — for the layout, the Received HSTACK spacer `flex:1` — earlier it was expanded to longhands and my collapse converted back. In the latest run, the Received shows `flex:1` already? The layout failure Received shows `<div data-pathland-id=13 style:flex:1></div>` — hmm, that means happy-dom serialized the spacer's `el.style.flex="1"` as `flex:1` this time?? But earlier (before my flex collapse normalizer) it showed `flex-basis:0%;flex-grow:1;flex-shrink:1`. Oh — my collapse normalizer now converts those longhands back to `flex:1`. So the Received shows `flex:1` because my normalizer collapsed it. Good, that's working.

Now the layout failure is ONLY the rgba formatting + maybe the flex:1 1 auto on COLOR (both already show flex:1 1 auto). Let me diff:
Expected: `align-self:stretch;background-color:rgba(255,0,0,1);color:rgba(255,0,0,1);flex:1 1 auto`
Received: `align-self:stretch;background-color:rgba(255, 0, 0, 1.000);color:rgba(255, 0, 0, 1.000);flex:1 1 auto`
Only the rgba formatting differs. So the rgba normalizer fixes layout.

For composite_controls:
1. select `value:1` vs `value:B` → fix picker option values (renumber).
2. gauge `width:42%` vs `width:4200%` → fix applyRangeBounds to set data-min/max on the gauge element itself.
3. divider `border-top:1px solid rgba(0,0,0,0.2)` vs Received `border-top-color:rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.2);border-top-style:solid;border-top-width:1px` — the runtime divider shell sets `el.style.borderTop = "1px solid rgba(0,0,0,0.2)"`; happy-dom expands border-top into longhands (border-top-color/style/width). SSR keeps `border-top:1px solid rgba(0,0,0,0.2)`. So I need a border-top collapse normalizer: if border-top-color/style/width present and no border-top shorthand, collapse to `border-top:{width} {style} {color}`. But the order in CSS shorthand is `border-top: width style color` = `1px solid rgba(...)`. Let me add to normalizeStyle:
   ```
