Wait — there's a subtlety with the gauge: `el.querySelectorAll(".pathland-stepper-range, .pathland-gauge")` — for the gauge element (`.pathland-gauge`), the querySelectorAll searches its descendants (the inner bar div, no class) → none. And `el` itself is the gauge. My `metas = [el, ...descendants]` and the check `meta.classList.contains(...)` → el matches gauge → sets data-min/max on the gauge element. Good.

But also — the stepper: el = `.pathland-stepper` (doesn't match gauge/stepper-range), descendants include `.pathland-stepper-range` → set. Good.

Now the picker renumber in apply.ts. Let me add a helper and call it after insertAt in the picker branch.

Actually, let me reconsider: rather than renumbering all options each insert, I can set the option's value at creation. But the index depends on position. For APPEND inserts it's `options.length - 1`. Simpler to renumber after each picker insert. Let me add:

```ts
function renumberPickerOptions(container: Node): void {
  if (!(container instanceof HTMLSelectElement)) return;
  Array.from(container.options).forEach((o, i) => { o.value = String(i); });
}
```

And call it in the picker branch of INSERT_CHILD and MOVE_CHILD after insertAt. But wait — the SELECTION handler uses selectedIndex now, so renumbering values won't disturb selection. And select.value in controlState will reflect the selected option's value. Good.

Hmm, but one concern: after hydration of SSR (delta test doesn't include picker), the options carry value="0/1/2" from SSR. If the client later renumbers... only for runtime-inserted picker children. Fine.

Let me add renumberPickerOptions and call it.

