Phase 1 done (core module + spec). Now Phase 2: create `role_spec.rs` in pathland-render-html.

Let me design it:

```rust
//! Canonical ROLE → semantic HTML element mapping (spec/OPCODE.md §semantic
//! properties), shared with the TypeScript DOM client: the Rust renderer
//! consumes these tables directly, and `pathland-ts-codegen` emits the TS
//! equivalents — so the semantic-tag / ARIA mapping cannot drift.

use pathland_core::constants::role;

/// Roles that map onto a native semantic HTML element. A role retags only a
/// generic `div`/`span` shell (see `GENERIC_COMPONENTS`); control components
/// keep their native element and are never retagged here.
pub struct RoleTag {
    pub role: u8,
    /// The semantic HTML tag.
    pub tag: &'static str,
    /// The equivalent ARIA role (used only when no semantic tag applies).
    pub aria: &'static str,
}

pub const ROLE_TAGS: &[RoleTag] = &[
    // Landmarks / structural.
    RoleTag { role: role::BANNER, tag: "header", aria: "banner" },
    RoleTag { role: role::NAVIGATION, tag: "nav", aria: "navigation" },
    RoleTag { role: role::MAIN, tag: "main", aria: "main" },
    RoleTag { role: role::CONTENT_INFO, tag: "footer", aria: "contentinfo" },
    RoleTag { role: role::COMPLEMENTARY, tag: "aside", aria: "complementary" },
    RoleTag { role: role::ARTICLE, tag: "article", aria: "article" },
    RoleTag { role: role::SECTION, tag: "section", aria: "region" },
    RoleTag { role: role::SEARCH, tag: "search", aria: "search" },
    RoleTag { role: role::LIST, tag: "ul", aria: "list" },
    RoleTag { role: role::LIST_ITEM, tag: "li", aria: "listitem" },
    RoleTag { role: role::PARAGRAPH, tag: "p", aria: "paragraph" },
    // Header (heading) → an <hN>. The level will be driven by the design
    // system's typography tokens once those land; interim default is <h2>.
    RoleTag { role: role::HEADER, tag: "h2", aria: "heading" },
    // Summary → a region landmark.
    RoleTag { role: role::SUMMARY, tag: "section", aria: "region" },
];

/// Roles with no dedicated element, mapped to an ARIA `role` attribute.
pub const ROLE_ARIA: &[(u8, &str)] = &[
    (role::BUTTON, "button"),
    (role::LINK, "link"),
    (role::TEXT, "text"),
    (role::IMAGE, "img"),
    (role::TEXT_FIELD, "textbox"),
    (role::SLIDER, "slider"),
    (role::TOGGLE, "switch"),
    (role::CHECKBOX, "checkbox"),
    (role::RADIO_BUTTON, "radio"),
    (role::STEPPER, "spinbutton"),
    (role::TAB, "tab"),
    (role::TAB_BAR, "tablist"),
    (role::GRID, "grid"),
    (role::SCROLL_VIEW, "scrollbar"),
    (role::ADJUSTABLE, "slider"),
    (role::MENU, "menu"),
];
```

Wait — there's a subtlety about `aria` in RoleTag. For roles with a semantic tag, the tag conveys the role, so we DON'T emit the aria attr. But some semantic tags need explicit aria: e.g. `<div role="listitem">`? No — `<li>` implies listitem. `<p>` implies paragraph. `<section>` needs an accessible name for `region` role semantics, but that's fine. Actually for SECTION, the ARIA is `region` but `<section>` implies `region` (when it has an accessible name). Fine, no attr.

Now `semantic_tag` and helpers:

```rust
/// The components whose shell is a generic `div`/`span` that a semantic role
/// may retag. Control components keep their native element.
pub const GENERIC_COMPONENTS: &[u16] = &[
    component_type::TEXT,
    component_type::VSTACK,
    component_type::HSTACK,
    component_type::LAZY_VSTACK,
    component_type::LAZY_HSTACK,
    component_type::ZSTACK,
    component_type::GRID,
    component_type::LAZY_VGRID,
    component_type::LAZY_HGRID,
    component_type::SCROLLVIEW,
    component_type::COLOR,
    component_type::SHAPE,
    component_type::SPACER,
];

/// The semantic tag a `(component, role)` pair resolves to, or `None` when the
/// element should keep its default shell tag.
pub fn semantic_tag(component: u16, role: u8) -> Option<&'static str> {
    if !GENERIC_COMPONENTS.contains(&component) {
        return None;
    }
    ROLE_TAGS.iter().find(|r| r.role == role).map(|r| r.tag)
}

/// The ARIA `role` attribute value for a role, if the effective element does
/// not already convey the role via a semantic/native tag.
pub fn aria_role(role: u8, effective_tag: Option<&'static str>) -> Option<&'static str> {
    // If a semantic tag is applied, the element conveys the role.
    if effective_tag.is_some() {
        return None;
    }
    ROLE_ARIA.iter().find(|(r, _)| *r == role).map(|(_, aria)| *aria)
}
```

