parts.text:
Rust side passes. Now Phase 3 TS: role retag morph + role-spec-driven PROP_ROLE/roleAttr.

Steps:
1. Update `classes.ts`:
   - Remove the local `roleAttr` map (or repurpose it to use role-spec). Replace PROP_ROLE handler to use `ariaRole(component,...)` — but classes.ts doesn't know the component (no componentByNode). The role retag must happen in apply.ts (has registry + componentByNode). So:
     - In apply.ts `applyStyle` SET_PROPERTY: intercept PROP_ROLE (component-aware): retag via `semanticTag(component, code)`, then set/remove the `role` attr via `ariaRole(component, code)`.
     - In classes.ts: keep a `applyRoleAttr(el, role)` that just sets/removes the role attribute given the resolved ARIA string (no component logic) — OR move the role attr handling fully into apply.ts and remove PROP_ROLE from classes.ts HANDLERS. Since `applyProperty` is also called from elsewhere (applyNumericProperty default path), removing PROP_ROLE from HANDLERS and handling it only in applyStyle is cleaner. But `applyNumericProperty` has the special cases and default → applyProperty. PROP_ROLE currently isn't a special case there, so it goes to applyProperty → HANDLERS[PROP_ROLE]. I'll add PROP_ROLE as a handled case in applyStyle (like NAV_DEPTH/NAV_CHROME/PROGRESS) and remove the classes.ts PROP_ROLE handler (so it doesn't double-apply). Actually — to be safe, let me keep classes.ts's roleAttr but replace its content to use the generated role-spec for the ARIA-only path... no, classes.ts lacks component. Cleanest: handle PROP_ROLE entirely in applyStyle (apply.ts), remove from classes.ts HANDLERS.

2. In apply.ts applyStyle, add a branch for PROP_ROLE (numeric):
```ts
} else if (propId === PROP_ROLE) {
  const code = enumCode(valueType, op.c);
  const comp = componentByNode.get(el) ?? 0;
  const tag = semanticTag(comp, code);
  if (tag && el.tagName.toLowerCase() !== tag) {
    const morphed = morphRole(el, tag, r);
    // applyStyle's `el` is a const; need to reassign for subsequent uses? The el is only used here.
  }
  const aria = ariaRole(comp, code);
  if (aria) {
    el.setAttribute("role", aria);
  } else {
    el.removeAttribute("role");
  }
}
```
But `el` is `const` in applyStyle. morphRole returns a new element; the registry must point to it. Subsequent SET_PROPERTY ops in the same batch targeting this node would look up `r.byId.get(op.a)` again (fresh each op), so it's fine — morphRole updates byId. And the retagged element is inserted in place of the old. Good.

Wait — how does applyStyle get `el`? `const node = r.byId.get(op.a); const el = node as HTMLElement;`. Each op re-reads byId. So after morphRole updates byId, later ops get the new element. 

3. morphRole function (like morphProgress):
```ts
function morphRole(el: HTMLElement, tag: string, r: DomRenderer): HTMLElement {
  const fresh = document.createElement(tag);
  for (const attr of Array.from(el.attributes)) {
    fresh.setAttribute(attr.name, attr.value);
  }
  while (el.firstChild) {
    fresh.appendChild(el.firstChild);
  }
  if (el.parentNode) {
    el.parentNode.replaceChild(fresh, el);
  }
  const id = Number(el.getAttribute("data-pathland-id"));
  if (id) {
    r.byId.set(id, fresh);
  }
  const comp = componentByNode.get(el);
  if (comp !== undefined) {
    componentByNode.set(fresh, comp);
  }
  return fresh;
}
```

But careful: the role attr — the old element may already have a `role` attribute (from a previous ARIA-only role or from the shell). When morphing to a semantic tag, we should remove the role attr (the tag conveys it). When we `ariaRole` returns null, we `el.removeAttribute("role")`. But morphRole copies the old attrs including possibly `role`. After morph, the new el has the copied `role` attr. Then I set/remove based on ariaRole. So order: morph (copy attrs incl role), then if aria null → removeAttribute("role") on the morphed element. Let me handle: after morph, apply the aria decision to the (possibly new) element.

Let me restructure the branch:
```ts
} else if (propId === PROP_ROLE) {
  const code = enumCode(valueType, op.c);
  const comp = componentByNode.get(el) ?? 0;
  const target = el;
  const tag = semanticTag(comp, code);
  let current: HTMLElement = target;
  if (tag && current.tagName.toLowerCase() !== tag) {
    current = morphRole(current, tag, r);
  }
  const aria = ariaRole(comp, code);
  if (aria) {
    current.setAttribute("role", aria);
  } else {
    current.removeAttribute("role");
  }
}
```

But `el` is const and used after the branch? The branch is in the SET_PROPERTY case, and after the if/else chain there's nothing using el. So fine.

`enumCode` — need to import from classes.ts? It's not exported. I need a local value decoder. In apply.ts there's already usage of `f32FromBits` and valueType. For ROLE (F32 enum code): `code = valueType === VAL_ENUM ? op.c & 0xff : Math.round(f32FromBits(op.c))`. The Rust side decodes as `f32::from_bits(*bits) as u8`. So code = Math.round(f32FromBits(op.c)) & 0xff basically. Let me add a small local helper or use the same logic as classes.ts enumCode. classes.ts has `enumCode(vt, c)` but not exported. I'll add a local helper in apply.ts.

Actually, apply.ts already imports f32FromBits. Let me add:
```ts
function roleCode(valueType: number, c: number): number {
  return valueType === VAL_ENUM ? c & 0xff : Math.round(f32FromBits(c)) & 0xff;
}
```
Need VAL_ENUM import.

4. Remove PROP_ROLE from classes.ts HANDLERS and remove the `roleAttr` function (or keep it unused? remove to avoid noUnusedLocals). Actually `roleAttr` is used only in PROP_ROLE handler. Remove both.

Wait — but there's the `applyToggleStyle` that sets `role` attr on checkbox input for TOGGLE_STYLE. That's component-internal, keep it.

Also — the `ROLE` import? apply.ts needs `semanticTag`, `ariaRole` from "./generated/role-spec". And PROP_ROLE, VAL_ENUM.

Let me also double-check: the conformance test fresh-DOM applies CREATE then SET_PROPERTY ROLE. For a generic component, after CREATE the element is a span/div; the ROLE delta morphs to the semantic tag. Then style deltas (none for role scenario) — but wait, in the semantics scenario, nodes might also have SET_TEXT and other props. Order in the batch matters: if SET_TEXT comes AFTER the role morph, setNodeText on the h2 works (h2 has textContent). If SET_TEXT comes BEFORE the role morph, the span has text, morph copies children (the text node) to the h2. Either order works because morphRole moves children.

But there's a subtlety: other properties applied to the element — e.g. if the scenario sets PADDING before ROLE, the span gets style; morph copies the style attr. Good. If AFTER, applies to h2. Good.

Now, the ARIA-only case: a generic shell + role LINK → no tag → morph doesn't fire → `ariaRole` returns "link" → setAttribute role=link. Good. But wait — ariaRole for a generic component with role LINK: LINK is not in ROLE_TAGS, it's in ROLE_ARIA → returns "link". Good.

A control + role e.g. Button component + role BUTTON: ariaRole returns null (NATIVE_ROLE_COMPONENTS) → removeAttribute("role") (button has none). Good.

Now, `NATIVE_ROLE_COMPONENTS` and `GENERIC_COMPONENTS` are emitted in role-spec.ts as number arrays. The `semanticTag`/`ariaRole` functions in role-spec.ts take (component, role). component = componentByNode value (u16). Good.

Let me now edit classes.ts to remove PROP_ROLE handler + roleAttr, and edit apply.ts to add the role handling + morph.

First classes.ts. Let me view the roleAttr + PROP_ROLE handler.

